## Testing

The `Authenticator` interface is the seam for tests: substitute a fake authenticator that returns a known header map, and assert your code calls the API the way you expect.

```go file
import "{{packageName}}/pkg"

type fakeAuth struct{}

func (fakeAuth) Host() string { return "https://api.example.com" }
func (fakeAuth) AuthHeaders() map[string]string {
	return map[string]string{"Authorization": "Bearer test-token"}
}
func (fakeAuth) QueryParams() map[string]string  { return nil }
func (fakeAuth) CookieParams() map[string]string { return nil }

func newClient() *{{packageName}}.Client {
	return {{packageName}}.New{{clientClassName}}(fakeAuth{}, nil)
}
```

## Error Handling

Every failure the SDK produces satisfies the `{{errorPrefix}}Error` interface, so one `errors.As` against it catches all of them. It has four direct kinds: `ApiError` (an HTTP response or a transport failure), `SerializationError` (a request or response body that cannot be encoded or decoded), `OAuth2ServerError` (the token endpoint answered with an error) and `OAuth2TokenError` (the token response was unusable). The last two surface from the first authenticated call, so they are siblings of `ApiError`, not subtypes of it: an `ApiError` branch alone lets them escape. The error hierarchy is:

- `{{errorPrefix}}Error` (root interface)
  - `SerializationError`
  - `OAuth2ServerError`
  - `OAuth2TokenError`
  - `ApiError`
    - `ClientError` (4xx)
      - `BadRequestError` (400)
      - `UnauthorizedError` (401)
      - `ForbiddenError` (403)
      - `NotFoundError` (404)
      - `ConflictError` (409)
      - `UnprocessableEntityError` (422)
      - `TooManyRequestsError` (429)
      - `PaymentRequiredError` (402)
      - `MethodNotAllowedError` (405)
      - `RequestTimeoutError` (408)
      - `GoneError` (410)
      - `PayloadTooLargeError` (413)
      - `UnsupportedMediaTypeError` (415)
      - `LockedError` (423)
    - `ServerError` (5xx)
      - `InternalServerError` (500)
      - `NotImplementedError` (501)
      - `BadGatewayError` (502)
      - `ServiceUnavailableError` (503)
      - `GatewayTimeoutError` (504)
    - `NetworkError` (no HTTP response, status 0)
      - `NetworkTimeoutError` (the request timed out, status 0)

