### Raw responses, timeouts and the request id

Each operation has a `...WithHTTPInfo` variant returning `*ApiResult[T]` (`StatusCode`, `Data`, `RawBody`, `Headers`). `Timeout` is one end-to-end budget in positive milliseconds. An `X-Request-ID` header is added to each request unless you set one.

### Credentials

`New{{clientClassName}}WithToken(host, accessToken, transportOptions)` resolves each credential once, when the client is built: the argument wins, then `{{envVarPrefix}}_BASE_URL` for the host and `{{envVarPrefix}}_TOKEN` for the token; an empty value counts as unset, and a credential that cannot be resolved returns `ErrMissingCredential`. `New{{clientClassName}}WithTokenProvider(host, provider, transportOptions)` takes a `func(ctx context.Context) (string, error)` that runs for every request.

`New{{clientClassName}}WithCredentials(host, {{packageName}}.Credentials{...}, transportOptions)` takes one optional authenticator per security scheme, named after the scheme; each operation uses the credential of the scheme its spec requires and there is no per-call override. Each security scheme has its own authenticator constructor, which resolves a credential the same way: a value you pass wins, an empty string is read once from the scheme's variable (an empty variable counts as unset), and a credential found in neither place returns an error matching `auth.ErrMissingCredential` that names the variable. A Bearer scheme also has `New<Scheme>AuthenticatorWithProvider(host, provider)`, which reads no variable.
{{#hasSecuritySchemeEnvVars}}

{{#securitySchemeEnvVars}}
- `{{envName}}`: the `{{field}}` of the `{{schemeId}}` scheme
{{/securitySchemeEnvVars}}
{{/hasSecuritySchemeEnvVars}}

### Per-call options

Every operation, also one with no parameters and no security, takes `RequestOption` values as its last arguments: `WithTimeout(time.Duration)`, `WithHeaders(map[string]string)`, `WithMaxRetries(int)` and, for an operation with several request content types, `WithRequestContentType(string)`. An option left out keeps the client's setting.

### Response compression, logging and versioning

Responses in `gzip`, `deflate`, `br` and `zstd` are decoded for you. The SDK writes no log lines: wrap the transport with `TransportOptionsBuilder.RoundTripper` to log requests. The module follows semantic versioning, and Go is supported from the oldest release that has not reached end of life.

### Hooks and telemetry

One optional hook runs around every call: `{{packageName}}.New{{clientClassName}}WithToken(host, token, nil, {{packageName}}.WithCallHook(hook))` (or `NewConfigurationBuilder().CallHook(hook)`). `CallHook.Start(ctx, info, headers)` runs once per call after the headers are final and may edit them; it returns a `CallObserver` (or `nil`) whose `Finish(outcome)` runs exactly once with the status (`0` when no response arrived), the lower-cased headers (`nil` when none arrived) and the error. An error from `Start` reaches the caller; a panic in `Finish` is recovered. `ChainCallHooks(a, b)` starts in order and finishes in reverse. `otelhook.New()` (package `pkg/otelhook`) records one span, `http.client.request.count` and `http.client.request.duration` per call; it needs `go.opentelemetry.io/otel` in your module.

## API Methods

Every operation takes a `ctx context.Context` as its first parameter; cancelling it aborts the request (`errors.Is(err, context.Canceled)`), and `client.Close()` is graceful and idempotent: requests in flight finish and new ones return `ErrClientClosed`. Streams that are still open are cancelled. Each API group is exposed as a typed field on the client (e.g., `client.{{#apiInfo}}{{#apis}}{{#-first}}{{#operations}}{{baseName}}{{/operations}}{{/-first}}{{/apis}}{{/apiInfo}}`). API classes have methods that correspond to OpenAPI operations, accepting typed request parameters and returning typed response models.

## Models

Models are generated as Go structs in the `models` package.

```go file
import "{{packageName}}/pkg/models"

{{#models}}{{#model}}{{#-first}}func accept(m models.{{classname}}) models.{{classname}} {
	return m
}{{/-first}}{{/model}}{{/models}}
```

## Binary / File Uploads

A raw binary request body is a `*BodySource` (see Streaming). Multipart file parameters are typed as `io.Reader`; an `*os.File` satisfies it, and so does `bytes.NewReader` or `strings.NewReader`. A binary response body is returned as a `*ByteStream`, which is an `io.ReadCloser` (see Streaming).

## Comment Style

Never place a comment on the same line as code. Use block comments (`/* ... */`).

```good
/* This explains the logic */
x := 1
```

```bad
// This explains the logic
x := 1
```
